## The judge is already part of this system

[READ N:gates/check.py; gates/fast.py; decisions/gates.md, GATES-19/20/22]
Nano has source gates, a selective fast path, full judges, and convergence. A fast landing builds
reached libraries/executables and selected cargo checks; it does not certify every runtime row,
census, or benchmark. Later convergence is a separate evidence event and must stay visible.

[READ N:gates/readset.py; gates/rebase.py, verify]
Traced keys record file reads and failed probes, compiler reads, selected stat information,
directory listings, executed binaries, tool versions, row text, and applicable waste limits.
The verifier recomputes keys on the candidate. Reuse requires a prior successful verdict with
the same key; red or incomplete work is not reusable successful evidence.

[READ N:gates/readset.py; decisions/gates.md, GATES-20]
The mechanism distinguishes runtime reads from compiler reads and listing/absence dependencies.
It records outside-tree observations without making every such input content part of the tree key;
the decision records identify external configuration, including git/lake configuration, as unfinished.
This is a concrete boundary on any claim that the whole judge already satisfies `ReadsOnly`.

[INFERRED] In closure language, a successful row is a candidate receipt, its input key supplies
supports, changed inputs withdraw old authorization, and re-running is a repair attempt.
The conservative fallback supplies a larger support set when tracing cannot justify narrowing.
Convergence adds overdue receipts and can discover conflicts that a fast landing never checked.

[INFERRED] Calling this an ATMS is useful if it means conditional evidence with retained reasons.
The present implementation does not yet provide a theorem-level ATMS with alternative proof routes,
negative semantic blockers, persistent unresolved obligations, or a proved recursive dependency adapter.
Its read sets are process/file dependencies, not an account of theorem premises.

[READ N:Census/Axioms.lean; gates/census.py]
The census walks declarations and collects axiom dependencies, checking the allowed standard axioms.
