[INFERRED] PRICE PREMISES — this memo and the proposed first tooling increment: landed theorems
gaining a hypothesis: **none**. Landed theorem statements restated: **none**. New adapter theorems
will have explicit coverage, revision-agreement, and support-sufficiency premises; these are new
obligations to discharge, not premises quietly attached to Kernel soundness or existing closure results.

[INFERRED] Price later semantic repairs separately. A collision repair or dependent-context redesign
may legitimately replace statements and redistribute assumptions. Before that change, enumerate
the affected landed theorem names, old/new telescopes, successor relations, and consumer discharges.
“The dependency graph touched N nodes” is not a substitute for that price.

[INFERRED] Tooling needs no Lang wire or Store flag day. Versioned journal records can coexist during
migration. The approved B constructor remains a separate Lang redesign; the tooling experiment can
start before it and should join it afterward through shared obligation interfaces, not a second
unrelated hole engine. No re-genesis cost is an argument for weakening the intended design.

[READ W:DECISIONS-20261021.md, CARD 9; N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/LangInstance.lean]
B changes the deployed term language and its checker/evaluator/parser/printer/wire inductions.
The old closed-fragment `langTemplate_no_hyps` boundary must remain explicitly fragment-scoped or
receive a replacement when the general instance changes. “No added premises” does not mean every
old boundary statement can be carried unchanged to a stronger language.

[INFERRED] ASK, implementation sequencing fork: receipt-first builds the graph adapter, proving
composition and revision correspondence while Lean remains the proof checker. Dependent-first
generalizes the contextual calculus to dependent telescopes before building the richer Lean-term bridge.
Both can reach the destination. Receipt-first estimates 4–7 engineer-days for the nano pilot;
dependent-first estimates 10–20 for a first bridge, with materially greater uncertainty.
These are planning estimates, not measured throughput, and not a reopening of CARD 9's Lang option B.

[INFERRED] Recommend receipt-first with an explicit dependent-context follow-on: it tests the actual
dependency and audit semantics before fixing them inside a richer calculus. This is an order of work,
not a smaller destination chosen for cost. Mini's existing exporters make its pilot an estimated
2–4 additional days; bread's first floor-component pilot an estimated 4–8. These exclude wholesale
semantic repairs and full-library recovery. Judge queues and source drift are extra elapsed time.

[INFERRED] Deliver the nano journal, exporter, checked adapter invariant, scratch mutation receipts,
and comparison report together. Then use mini's real constructor redesign and bread's real premise
flow as acceptance cases. The result should let an owner withdraw a definition or audit assumption
and receive a concrete, priced repair program whose eventual claims are checked against fresh evidence.

