decisions/gates.md:432:## GATES-19 · The landing queue: behind-main is a judged merge, readiness order with an age bound, verdicts carried only under a byte-identical input key (2026-10-19/20, ROOT rulings; design docs/record/GATES-19-DESIGN.md)
decisions/gates.md:457:- queue (land.py on a scratch ref, tickets from gate-lib.sh new_ticket): a lander still judging blocks no ready one (`queue: head (displaced 0 times)`); a twice-displaced live ticket makes a ready younger lander print `queue: waiting for 00000003.2204417 (displaced 2): displaced 2 times, it is head regardless (GATES-19)`, the ref does not move until the old one lands, and the younger is then displaced (75, count 1); a dead head (holder stopped by its PID) is skipped and the waiter lands; the age bound: displaced on passes 1 and 2, then `A pass 3 exit=0` while a younger ready D waits and is displaced after; a stale base is still refused by the CAS.
decisions/gates.md:465:**Measured first day (GATES-8, 2026-10-19, every scripts/commit log on hbox under the new queue).** 13 landers logged `queue: head (displaced N)`: N=0: 5, N=1: 1, N=2: 6, N=3: 1 (HOST-61, bd39f78). Reaching the bound is the common case (6 of 13). Every pass beyond 3 was a TIE, which the age bound allows: two landers twice-displaced at once (tickets 21, 22 at c7f455e), then four (28, 33, 35, 36 at bd39f78 → 224e9fe; OPS-18 oldest). k tied landers landing serially onto each other's tips cost the youngest k−1 more re-bases by construction (each is judged on the tip it lands onto), so no ordering rule bounds that below 2 + k; ROOT withdrew a 4-pass rule on this. What shortens a tie re-base is reuse (GATES-20); a merge-train is GATES-21, PROPOSED (docs/record/GATES-21-PROPOSED.md), built only if the measured tail stays long under GATES-20. Admission control (refusing entry while ties stand) is refused: it shortens nothing a lander can see and starves the newest.
decisions/gates.md:467:## GATES-20a · A run is selected by every binary it runs, not only its own: spawning rows, declared drives, and names in the static key (2026-10-20, ROOT ruling; found by GATES-8's traced read-sets)
decisions/gates.md:472:- GATES-19's critique (e) said no exe walks the tree; `cargo:devtools:workspace` does: it stats every entry under the root, decisions/ included (`crates()`), so a `decisions/x/Cargo.toml` changes its verdict while GATES-19's static key held no decisions/ path at all.
decisions/gates.md:484:**Critique.** (a) Spawning rows select on every exe's closure: rows and store-plants run on nearly every Lean landing. They are seconds, and the traced read-set (GATES-20) is what narrows them to what they actually run. (b) The lean_bin scan reads string literals; an exe name built at run time (`format!`) escapes it, and only a trace sees that: the standing trace should be a check, not a measurement (GATES-20). (c) The tests/common over-approximation names drives a test never runs.
decisions/gates.md:485:**Meta-critique.** GATES-19 keyed reuse on "every binary" precisely because a run may drive any other, and never asked the same question of selection: the key and the selection answered "what can move this run" differently, and only the key was right. The invariant now stated: a row is selected whenever a binary it may run can change.
decisions/gates.md:487:## GATES-20b · The landed copy: every path is merged from what the lander recorded, never taken from the working tree; a landing that would revert a recent commit is refused `stale path` unless named; a head lander is never charged for a tie (2026-10-21, ROOT rulings; HOSTING-13's hazard, LANG-21's finding)
decisions/gates.md:489:**Why.** HOSTING-13 caught by hand, mid-judge, that a re-based attempt re-read `launcher/tests/members.rs` from the working tree while CLIENT-31 (cdc0e9a) had added 70 lines to it on main. Verified at source (hbox logs/gates9-verify.sh; a scratch worktree, main's own judge with a stub pre-push that prints the candidate and refuses, so nothing could land): GATES-19's merge ran only for a path whose copy at the worktree's HEAD differed from main's, with HEAD's copy as the base, and the candidate was then `git add -A` of the working copy. HEAD stands in for "the copy the lander edited", and nothing checks that. **V1**: a copy made from a1ca640 plus one lane line, written into a worktree at main (a scratch copy copied in, the scp workflow several lanes use): candidate `members.rs | 81 +++----`, 1003 lines, CLIENT-31's lines gone and the lane line in. **V2**: worktree at a1ca640, lane line added, then `git reset main` (HEAD moved, the file left): the same 1003-line candidate. **V0** (control): HEAD left at a1ca640: merged, 1077 lines. A green judge lands V1 and V2. HOSTING-39 itself (ea52a4a) was safe: its HEAD stayed at a1ca640, so its attempt 5 merged. How GATES-19 missed it: its plants put the lane's HEAD at the lane's real base each time, the one case where HEAD is right. The rewrite case was also open: the working file was re-read on every attempt, so a file rewritten between attempts (P4 below) landed whole.
decisions/gates.md:492:1. **The landed copy comes from a record, never from the working tree** (gates/rebase.py `snapshot`/`candidate`). The lander's first attempt records, per file under its PATH..., BASE = the blob at the worktree's HEAD then and OURS = the working copy then (written to the object store), in `<lander dir>/snapshot.tsv`. Every attempt builds its candidate from that record against main's blob M: OURS = M nothing; OURS = BASE (not edited) main's copy; BASE = M (main did not change it) OURS; otherwise `git merge-file OURS BASE M`, a conflict refused by name with its hunks, an absent side refused. The working tree is not read again during the landing. After the compare-and-swap `writeback` writes the landed copy into each working file that still holds OURS. A file edited while the lander ran keeps that edit, merged 3-way onto the landed copy (base = OURS), as an uncommitted edit, or is named when it does not merge. GATES-19's merged.tsv is deleted, because the record makes it unnecessary.
decisions/gates.md:509:**What the old judge says about this commit:** main's scripts/commit and gates/rebase.py build its candidate (the HEAD-based merge) and main's pre-push judges it; its paths are GATES' own. **What the new judge would have said:** the same candidate. wt/gates was detached at main before launch (ROOT's mitigation), so its record equals main plus these four files, and `stale` finds no revert in them (rebase.py's old `merge` is deleted, the block GATES-19 added: that is 6527d9f's/1a6163b's hunk being replaced, which GATES names for itself).
decisions/gates.md:512:**Meta-critique.** GATES-19 trusted HEAD as "the copy the lander edited" and its plants never moved HEAD. The defect lived in what the plants held fixed. This decision's own blind spot is the window; the measure that would expose it is the age of the oldest copy a lane copies in, which nothing records.
decisions/gates.md:514:## GATES-20 · Traced read-sets select the runs and key the reuse; the judge's code is main's, the candidate supplies only its table (2026-10-20, landed 10-21 after GATES-20b; ROOT rulings F1/F2 and "the judge's CODE is main's"; design docs/record/GATES-20-DESIGN.md)
decisions/gates.md:520:  1. its reach (GATES-20a) meets the changed .lean modules;
decisions/gates.md:527:- **The judge's code is main's.** pre-push runs `$J/runs.py` and `$J/waste.py` (J = the judging scripts' tree: main's copy under scripts/commit). The candidate supplies only its runs.tsv and waste.tsv; a candidate's gates/*.py is never executed by its own judge. Consequence (ROOT): a new row KIND and the first rows that use it are two commits, runner first. Why: GATES-19 (1a6163b) and GATES-20a (6527d9f) both changed gates/runs.py and were judged by their own runner. Standing on each read GREEN (1a6163beb767, 6527d9f146e0), and that was the only check after the fact.
decisions/gates.md:529:- **Reads outside the tree (F2) are reported, not RED.** runs.py prints `outside EXE: home N, tmp M`, and standing's line carries `outside=N(traced|untraced)`. **GATES-20c** turns it RED. Trigger: the census below is sorted. Dated: 2026-10-27.
decisions/gates.md:533:  - selected runs 3025 (static, GATES-20a) → **1922**;
decisions/gates.md:545:  GATES-20c's first item is to sort those three: key them, or make the runs not read them, with HOME pointed at the run's fresh dir.
decisions/gates.md:558:  - R2 `g20-data`: B0 = main plus world/plant-g20.txt (a file nothing reads), M = one comment line of Ledger/Sim/Journey.lean. Result: `PLANT g20-data GREEN (control)`, 24 units reused and 2 re-run. The re-runs were census and cargo:devtools:workspace, which lists world/ and saw the added path. GATES-19's static key re-ran every row (g19-data).
decisions/gates.md:566:- The re-trace check is new red. The outside report is new information, and RED is dated as GATES-20c.
decisions/gates.md:572:- (d) The outside census found three real inputs no key covers. Until GATES-20c, a change to ~/.gitconfig can move a verdict that a re-base then carries.
decisions/gates.md:578:- Built by GATES-8 on 6527d9f and saved uncommitted while GATES-20b (8c2c5fd) landed first (ROOT: the silent revert outranked it); re-applied --3way by GATES-9 onto main with no conflict. runs.py, pre-push and standing.sh are byte-identical to the planted copies (logs/gates9-saved-20/), and rebase.py differs only by GATES-20b, which does not touch keys or verify. So the plants above stand for the landed code. The outside-reads RED, called GATES-20b in ROOT's ruling, is renumbered GATES-20c because GATES-20b is the landed copy.
decisions/gates.md:580:## GATES-20d · A compile's reads are C, compared only against a compile; an escaped name invalidates the trace; an implied directory is not an extra (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; standing 145283a RED)
decisions/gates.md:582:**Why.** The first standing after GATES-20 (145283aa3e71) read RED: `runs=RED(cargo:attach:attach (re-trace): RED(trace: the run touched attach/src (+14 more), which a stored read-set valid on this tree omits …))`, on 13 of the 14 cargo rows. Every row's own verdict was ok. Measured over the store (hbox logs/gates9-rs.py, gates9-rs2.py): the stored read-sets valid on that tree came from judges whose cargo cache was warm (attach: 76 touched paths), and standing compiled (91). Every extra on every cargo row was a directory or an ABSENT `X/mod.rs` probe, read only when rustc runs: rustc tries `X.rs` and `X/mod.rs` for each `mod X;`, and cargo resolves each dep-info path's ancestors after a compile (`readlink launcher/tests` = EINVAL). GATES-20 assumed that a warm cargo reads less and never more. That is true, but the check assumed the stored record was the larger one, and the judges' warm records are the smaller. So it read RED on every standing that compiled, which is red as steady state. GATES-8's determinism plant missed it because its second pass was the warm one.
decisions/gates.md:599:**Blind spot (ROOT: in the decision AND dated under GATES-20c).** A warm cargo, judge and standing alike, cannot see a file ADDED at a rustc `mod` probe path (`client/src/attempt/mod.rs` beside `attempt.rs`, or a new `X/mod.rs` for a `mod X;` that resolved to `X.rs`), because dep-info lists only the files the compile read. A warm read-set inherits that: its C is empty, so the probe paths are in no key. GATES-20 did not cause this; it inherits it from cargo's fingerprint.
decisions/gates.md:600:**GATES-20c, item 2, dated 2026-10-27**: close it. The mitigations, priced:
decisions/gates.md:606:**Meta-critique.** Planted with two passes, both warm-after-cold, the check passed. Run in the wild against a warm record, it read red at once. The plant held the cache order fixed, the way GATES-19's plants held HEAD fixed: the same blind spot, a week apart.
decisions/gates.md:608:## GATES-20e · A judge table is an input of every row it judges: a changed waste ceiling selects the row that measured it, standing-only included (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; PERF-4's fail-open at 41e041f)
decisions/gates.md:610:**Finding (fail-open under GATES-20, 145283a).** PERF-4's landing 41e041f re-pinned worstcut ceilings in gates/waste.tsv. Attempt 1, judged before GATES-20, ran standing:bench-worstcut and bench-worstworld GREEN against the new ceilings. Attempts 2–6 printed `NOT RUN (standing-only; its inputs are unchanged)`. The bench never READS gates/waste.tsv: the JUDGE compares the bench's `WASTE` lines against it. So no trace holds it, and the traced read-set made the ceiling invisible to selection. A ceiling edit landed without the row it changes being judged. This is the same class as the self-judge finding: a table the judge reads to form a verdict was outside what the verdict was keyed on.
decisions/gates.md:621:**Plants** (hbox logs/gates9-20e.py/.out, function-level over runs.traced_selected on the standing tree at 145283a with its gates/waste.tsv edited and restored under the standing lock; main's judge = GATES-20, new = this commit's runs.py/readset.py; the store a copy with W set per case):
decisions/gates.md:628:## GATES-20f · A dead lander's scratch is reaped at a later lander's start, by liveness only (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; PROTOCOL-21's 709572)
decisions/gates.md:640:**GATES-20e fix (landed with GATES-20f).** 20e's selection read `wchg & set(rs["W"])` on every row whose valid read-set has no W key, including when gates/waste.tsv had NOT changed. Every record stored before 20e has no W, so runs.py crashed with `KeyError: 'W'` in traced_selected, and pre-push refused with no verdict line (`runs: NO VERDICT`/`RED`). That refused every landing after c8ebb06 whose change met a pre-20e record (KERNEL-23, PROTOCOL-21, this lane's own GATES-20f). It failed closed: nothing landed on it. The cause was that every 20e plant changed waste.tsv, the one case where the branch above guards the read. Fix: the W branch runs only when a waste row changed (`wchg and …`); a record without W is still taken by the "does not say what it measures" branch whenever one did. Plant (hbox logs/gates9-20e-fix.py/.out; a change to Kernel/Proofs/InvokeTree.lean only, over the real store): main (c8ebb06) `KeyError: 'W' (elif wchg & set(rs["W"]):)`; this one `ok, 2 rows selected`. The 20e plants (logs/gates9-20e.py) are unchanged by it.
decisions/gates.md:642:## GATES-20b′ · A prior commit's change is contained when the copy holds it, whatever its context (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; PERF-6's false positive)
decisions/gates.md:644:**Why.** `stale` (GATES-20b) read a commit C's change as CONTAINED in a copy X only when `git merge-file X pre_C post_C` was clean and left X unchanged. 35daeab (PERF-8) added the bench-worstworld block to lakefile.lean, using the bench-worstcut lines as its context. PERF-12 deletes those lines and keeps the block unchanged. The merge re-anchors on the context that survives and inserts the block a SECOND time. X "changes", so lacks() read LACKING, and PERF-6's landing was refused `stale path: lakefile.lean: the landing would revert 35daeab's change` (hbox logs/perf6-commit1.log, reproduced by logs/perf6-lacks.py). Any deletion of a prior commit's context lines tripped it. That is a false refusal: the copy reverts nothing.
decisions/gates.md:654:## GATES-22 · The fast landing and the convergence: a landing is judged by what it reaches, the whole system every 3 h, a red names the commits it read (2026-10-21, Ember's ruling via ROOT)
decisions/gates.md:656:**Why.** Ember, 10-21: "we don't need to focus on landings as much, as long as every now and then we make sure the whole system still builds and passes its tests, and do convergences at those points instead of EVERY LANDING." The numbers before (GATES-10, hbox logs/gates10-fluid-measure.out and the landing logs): **61 re-judges in 24 h for 57 landings**, each a full judge (every target, the census, every selected row, the waste, the generated files, the Rust workspace with clippy); a standing pass **41 min** (fc4022d); GATES-20b′'s own landing **~2.5 h through the queue**, displaced twice, and refused once by a **30-min slot starvation** that read `REFUSED by scripts/pre-push`; on 10-21 evening all four slots were held by full judges for **45 min straight** (ROOT), and this lane's own first two plant judges waited 30 min each and got no slot (the second already printing NOT JUDGED, item 6). GATES-20j itself (a 3-file `gates:` change): judged GREEN, displaced; GREEN, displaced; then REFUSED by the 30-min slot wait, ~2 h after launch, with nothing wrong in it. Of 60 landings in the 24 h to cdd9d84, 7 were flag days and 8 changed the judge's code (7 of them GATES' own); the other ~45 were judged in full for changes the next standing judged again anyway.
decisions/gates.md:660:   - the stale-path guard (GATES-20b/20b′, in scripts/commit, unchanged) and every source gate, the candidate's and the parent's (gates/check.py: owners, closure, refusals, size, paths, the runs table, tcb, vectors, the waste ratchet, targets);
decisions/gates.md:664:   - The landed message carries `JUDGED: fast (GATES-22) — (built: …; cargo: …)`, which the convergence reads.
decisions/gates.md:668:   - every verdict row the change selected (sims, journeys, plants, cargo rows, script rows: THE BAR), with their known-red controls and traces, and the standing-only benches GATES-19 ran in pre-push when their inputs changed;
decisions/gates.md:673:   - GATES-19's keyed reuse and the PRE-EXISTING parent re-judge exist only in the full judge, where they still apply.
decisions/gates.md:677:4. **The revert rule.** A unit still red at the next convergence (a stage that did not run proves nothing and changes no count), whose commit is attributed both at its first sighting and now, and is the only one: scripts/converge lands its revert by name, a `gates:` landing through scripts/commit with `--reverts C` (GATES-20b: the REVERTS: hunk in the message) and C's owners in CROSS-LAYER. Several attributed: `REVERT-PENDING` names them and ROOT runs `scripts/converge --revert C`. A commit is reverted once; a revert that does not apply cleanly is `REVERT-FAILED`, for ROOT.
decisions/gates.md:680:7. **The lanes' adds (ROOT 10-21, from every lane's measured answer).** (a) THE SOURCE GATES FIRST: every judge, fast or full, runs gates/check.py (the candidate's and the parent's: owners and CROSS-LAYER, SIZE lines, the waste and ceiling ratchets, closure, …) on a private copy BEFORE it asks for a slot; a red is refused in seconds with the lines to add (a lane waited 30 min for a slot to learn it had indented a line). (d) DOCS-ONLY: a change that reaches no Lean target and no Rust package (README.md, CHARTER.md, docs/, decisions/, gates/doc-allow.tsv and every gates/*.tsv, world texts, deploy/, generated docs) is the no-slot fast path: the source gates only, seconds. Before 22 CHARTER.md, README.md and gates/doc-allow.tsv selected every row as paths a run may read (a one-line doc-allow commit cost 70 min; a docs-only ops: commit 2–4 h); now no row runs for them per landing, and a row that reads them is judged at the convergence, where every row runs. (f) KEEP THE PLACE: a judge whose slots are all busy waits on, naming the holders every 30 min, and is NOT JUDGED (exit 76) only after 4 h (ND_SLOT_WAIT); a full lander keeps its ticket meanwhile (HOSTING-16 lost its place behind 9 landers). (c) THE DISJOINT RE-MERGE is what the fast path already is: a displaced fast lander is re-merged from its record and re-judged by the gates and its reached build, never by rows (seconds to minutes); a FULL lander keeps GATES-19's keyed reuse. (b) APPEND-ONLY MERGE (gates/rebase.py candidate, the directories in gates/append-only.tsv: a table, since the judge code names no doc-allow path itself): a decisions/*.md that both the lander and main only APPENDED to since the lander's base lands as main's copy with the lander's addition after it, never a conflict (KERNEL-26 was refused on decisions/protocol.md and trial-merged every main commit by hand); an edit above the end on either side is merged and refused as before (plant hbox logs/gates11-append-plant.out: both appended → `a b main mine`; an edit above → the conflict; main's rebase.py on the same append → the conflict). (e) one retry of a row's flake (printed FLAKE by row, counted by standing) loosens a check, so it is its own `gates:` commit with its own decision, right after this one.
decisions/gates.md:681:8. **Not needed: GATES-21 (merge train) and 20i (batch integration).** Both existed to make a full judge per landing affordable; with no full judge per landing, nothing is left for them to batch. **20g (concurrent standing rows) is next**: the convergence is now the only full run, and its 41-min wall is the whole system's latency to a verdict.
decisions/gates.md:690:**Critique.** (a) A fast landing that breaks a row it does not build lands, and main is red until the next convergence finds it (≤3 h plus ~45 min) and its owner fixes it, or until the convergence after that reverts it (≤~7 h): that latency is the price Ember chose, stated. Landings on a red main still land fast; the range grows and attribution narrows it. (b) Attribution is as good as the read-sets: an input no trace holds (outside reads, GATES-20c) attributes to nothing (`no commit of the range (ROOT)`); a row with no stored read-set falls to the static rule and over-attributes; the build names only modules its errors name. (c) K by kind: a non-cargo program that branches on a stat's size or mtime of a tree file is not keyed by it (ROOT's ruling for 20h (a)). (d) The revert is automatic only for exactly one commit attributed at both sightings; a red that two commits make together is ROOT's. (e) The fast build compiles lib targets whole and follows imports only: a file a module reads at elaboration time is not traced to its targets (the convergence's full build is). (f) A fast lander waits up to 60 min behind a twice-displaced full lander. (g) The first convergence after this commit seeds key v4 and runs every row; its re-trace check compares nothing that pass.
decisions/gates.md:692:**GATES-22 fix (2026-10-21, KERNEL-28's crash).** The outer loop read `$ND_TICKET` before every attempt (`before=$(cat "$ND_TICKET" …)`, for land.py --charge). A FAST lander holds no ticket, so under `set -u` every fast attempt printed `ND_TICKET: unbound variable`. In KERNEL-28's landing, after a FAST GREEN displaced by e568634→b50da06, the lander's directory was gone and rebase.py's snapshot failed (`snapshot.tsv.tmp: No such file`): kernel28-commit1/2.log. The read is now `${ND_TICKET:-/dev/null}`. Plant (hbox logs/gates11-fastfix-plant.sh/.out, the real outer loop in a scratch clone, stub pre-push): main's loop prints `line 119: ND_TICKET: unbound variable` before every attempt of F1 and of the displaced F2; this one prints none, and F2 re-bases once and lands. The harness reproduces the unbound read, not the directory loss KERNEL-28 saw after it; that loss follows the same read and is gone with it. My GATES-22 plants printed the same line, and their summary grep did not look for it.
decisions/gates.md:694:**GATES-22 (b) fix: the LANDER's copy decides an append (2026-10-21).** 22's rule needed both sides to be pure appends since the lander's base. b50da06 (ROOT) edited GATES-19/20 headings above the end of decisions/gates.md, so 20j, which had only appended, was refused with a conflict at its re-base (logs/gates11-commit20j-3.log). The rule now reads only the lander's copy: if it is its base plus a tail, the landed copy is main's as it is (whatever main changed, above the end or at it) followed by that tail. A lander that edited above the end is merged, and refused on overlap, as any file. Plant (hbox logs/gates11-append2-plant.out; scratch repo, main edits line 1 and appends, the lander appends): main's rebase.py `REFUSED — merge conflict`, this one `A b c main mine`; a lander edit above the end: still the conflict.
decisions/gates.md:696:## GATES-20k · A failure of the tracer is not a verdict: UNREAD(tracer), re-run once, twice NOT JUDGED; a retry that passes is a counted flake (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; GATES-10 verified at source)
decisions/gates.md:698:**Why.** The GATES-20 tracer sometimes fails mid-run: `strace: ptrace(PTRACE_LISTEN, …): Input/output error`. The run then exits 1 and runs.py read RED. It refused KERNEL-26's `kernel!` (gate-2/4c04f20aa738.log) and another lander's broker row (gate-3/2b29a0426731.log). Those were false refusals, never false passes. Under GATES-22 the convergence would read the same failure as a red row and attribute it to commits that did nothing.
decisions/gates.md:706:## GATES-20j · A skipped cargo test is never ok: INCOMPLETE in pre-push, RED in standing; `cargo-untraced:` rows run without the tracer (2026-10-21, ROOT ruling; HOSTING-15's fence, built and planted by GATES-10)
decisions/gates.md:708:**Why.** HOSTING-15's two-uid fence checks (a test that `sudo`s to the deployed uids) skip themselves when the fence is absent (no nd-hosted uid, /run cleared by a reboot): the test prints `skipped: …` and returns, and libtest counts it PASSED. A box without the fence would read every such row `ok`: the fail-open shape (an absent verdict read as a pass). And such a test cannot run under the GATES-20 tracer at all: strace --seccomp-bpf sets no_new_privs, so sudo refuses (measured on hbox 10-21: `sudo -n -u nd true` exit 0 plain, exit 1 traced).
decisions/gates.md:712:- A row `cargo-untraced:CRATE:TEST` is a cargo row run WITHOUT the tracer. It holds no read-set, so it is selected by the static rules alone (its crate, its drives' closures, every non-.lean change) and carried only under GATES-19's static key (every non-.lean source, the built binaries, the toolchain).
decisions/gates.md:713:Under GATES-22 a row runs at the convergence (standing: a skip is RED) and on a full landing (pending); a fast landing runs no row.
decisions/gates.md:719:## GATES-20g · Rows run concurrently on free judge slots, each in its own scratch; GATES-20l · the convergence carries what the delta cannot reach, for at most a day (2026-10-21, Ember's question via ROOT; GATES-11 measured, GATES-12 built)
decisions/gates.md:740:- *A read-set traced in a mount namespace never narrows and never carries.* This tightens GATES-20 and closes a fail-open candidate. A sandboxed process reads through mounts and names what it reads in its own view; the measured read-sets hold `launcher/newroot`, `client/newroot`. So its read-set may miss a box path its verdict depends on. Such a record no longer narrows selection (valid_readset) and is never reused or carried. The same holds for a record from before GATES-20g, which does not say whether it was traced in a namespace. The static rules apply. The in-namespace path is the SYMPTOM. The proper key for those rows is the HOST view of what bwrap mounted: trace the parent's mounts, or translate newroot paths through the bind table. That is GATES-20m (cv 01a15816-7439), and it would also let them run beside others (ROOT approved this containment inside 20g).
decisions/gates.md:743:- *Rows.* Standing runs runs.py with `--reuse <G>/carry.record --record <G>/carry.new --daily ND_DAILY` (default 86400). A row whose traced key is byte-identical on this tree is not run; its lines and WASTE lines are carried (`  carry ROW: carried (key …, byte-identical to its run at TREE TIME)`). The key covers every path it touched, stat'ed, listed or exec'd, its row line, its waste rows, the runner and the tools. The rule is GATES-19's reuse key, now also used in standing. A record whose run is older than a day is re-run (`  daily ROW: re-run …`). The re-trace check is what catches a narrowed record, and only a run can make it, so every row runs at least daily. Only ok rows are recorded, so a red is never carried.
decisions/gates.md:785:**GATES-20k fix: the tracer's own line as the box prints it (2026-10-21, PROTOCOL-23's refusal; GATES-12 verified at source).** 20k matched `^strace: `, so it never caught the failure it was written for. strace names itself by its argv[0], the absolute path readset.available() found, and writes on the same line as the tracee's unterminated output: `........../usr/bin/strace: ptrace(PTRACE_LISTEN,pid:…,sig:0): Input/output error`. All three hbox occurrences have that shape: gate-2/4c04f20aa738.log:860 and gate-3/2b29a0426731.log:412, which are 20k's own motivating cases, and gate-3/846590c46570.log:301, PROTOCOL-63's false refusal at about 04:30. 20k's plant used a fake strace that printed `strace: ` at a line start, so it checked its own fake and not the box's output. TRACER is now `(?m)(?:^|/)strace: .*$`.

