## What the landed theory actually gives us (rev 3)

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean] An interface names a hole, its context and type, token-backed hypotheses, guard, budget, chooser, and acceptor.
A candidate retains its filling, declared supports, blockers, cost, proposer, and status. Effective supports also include tokens for context
variables the filling actually uses. Tokens have identities and versions.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, judge] Judgment first checks typing, then the interface guard, then live blockers, then missing supports, then
budget. Its statuses distinguish ill-typed or guard-conflicting candidates, pruning, suspension, uncovered cost, and verification. These statuses are
distinct from the five outcomes of repairing a selected filling.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, provenance; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Judge.lean] The provenance representation is a list of
positive-support/negative-blocker pairs, one per candidate passing typing, guard, and cost. Alternatives form a sum; simultaneous supports form a
product. It is independent of which tokens are live, but depends on the environment, candidate set, and checker.

[INFERRED] This is useful signed provenance, not yet an arbitrary symbolic polynomial in all changing facts. In particular, guard failures are
filtered for the supplied environment. Retain the candidate archive as well as its currently computed polynomial, or a later environment change loses
alternatives. Do not flatten a shared proof DAG into exponentially many monomials merely to print this notation.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Judge.lean, withdraw_never_refutes] Withdrawal changes support availability, not the type or guard calculation; it
does not create a typing/guard conflict. The ill-typed conflict theorem refutes the proposed filling's typing judgment. It does not prove that the
hole's target proposition is false or that no other filling exists.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Repair.lean, repair_agrees_from_scratch; N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, observe/fromScratch] Under coherence, repair
and fresh judging produce equal recorded candidate-status lists. They use the same template and retained candidates. The observation excludes
selections, accepted flags, history, and owed obligations.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Repair.lean, locality/repair_rejudged_eq_touched] An unrelated token edit leaves the closure unchanged, and the repair
log contains exactly touched candidates. Environment replacement touches all candidates. The definitions still scan lists to find affected entries;
the logged-work theorem is not a bound saying elapsed work is O(affected entries).

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, repairSelected; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Outcomes.lean] The five outcomes are kept, alternative, replacement,
strengthening, and suspended. An alternative with the same filling can change the support route; a different replacement is offered for selection.
Strengthening supplies a smaller-context witness; it does not rewrite the closure's template.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Outcomes.lean, strengthening_sound; History.lean, session_preserves_tpl] The strengthening law needs a lawful
calculus, the relevant checked filling, and an unused variable. It reconstructs the original filling by weakening the smaller one. Sessions preserve
the template. Consequently, changing a theorem's telescope is not already one of the existing edit operations.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/ClosureStatements.lean, Template.Typed/Derivable/Available/Settled] Derivable means the partially filled template
types under the remaining hole declarations. Availability checks selected supports, and settledness quantifies over selections; an empty selection
set can satisfy these conditions. None alone says every intended obligation has a complete proof.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Soundness.lean, conditional_soundness_closure] The theorem requires a typed template, coherence, and settledness. The
operations do not establish template typing from arbitrary data. This is the same missing producer obligation identified in W:ACTIVITIES-LOG.md; a
