## What the raw documents add to the mechanism (rev 3)

[READ R:doc 1 §§2–8; doc 2 §§5–7; doc 3 §§4,7–8] The raw proposal requires more than a success-dependency graph: interaction obligations, four
preservation contracts, separated alternative worlds, grounded cycles, composable repair objects, polarity, scoped transport, and explicit logical
statuses. These requirements survive the source audit. Revision 3 makes them part of the proposed mechanism, rather than leaving them as literature
analogies. The supplied raw documents are the source for literature descriptions here; the linked papers have not been independently re-audited.

[READ R:doc 1 §5] The ludics proposal interprets a guard through admissible interactions: `F(h,w) = admissible ∩ T(h,w)^⊥`. Adding a required
counter-interaction intersects the eligible fillings with its orthogonal. If tested interactions are only a subset of required interactions, passing
the tests gives an overapproximation of admissibility. The raw text also warns that a daimon-like object which gives up can satisfy an interaction
criterion without completing a program.

[INFERRED] For proof engineering, let the obligation owner fix the guard: the exact target and context, intended instance, allowed assumptions, and
evidence required of consumers and audits. A candidate supplies evidence against that guard; it cannot replace it with an easier one. Treat ludics as
a design lens here, not a claim that Lean checking or nano's Boolean guard already implements orthogonality or biorthogonal closure. A finite plant
suite is evidence about the instrument; it is not completeness of the semantic audit. A suspended hole must never count as an established theorem,
even if a partial template remains derivable.

[READ R:doc 1 §6; N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/History.lean, consumer_boundary] The raw boundary theorem distinguishes four reuse claims. Nano's
`consumer_boundary` proves preservation for a consumer factoring through a specified projection when that projection is equal. It does not discover
that projection or prove arbitrary clients factor through it.

[INFERRED] Each consumer edge should therefore identify its preservation contract explicitly: (1) typing/interface preservation reuses client typing;
(2) logical-contract preservation reuses client proofs under that contract; (3) observable-behavior preservation reuses cached observations; (4)
cost/resource preservation reuses bounds and scheduling evidence. These are distinct promises, not a single unqualified “compatible” flag.
Binary-to-linear search can preserve membership and lose logarithmic cost. A proof-body replacement can preserve the target and lose reconstruction
performance; a representation equivalence can preserve meaning without preserving definitional equality.

[INFERRED] The boundary certificate should name old/new revisions, permitted observations, transport or equality evidence, and every residual
obligation. Dependent clients may mention the replaced inhabitant in their types; “both have type A” is insufficient. Prop proof irrelevance does not
make a changed executable definition, a new telescope, or a changed deployment instance interchangeable. A boundary with no checked transport
propagates repair to consumers instead of claiming locality.

[READ R:doc 2 §5] Interface automata supplies the input/output variance lesson through alternating simulation: an implementation must accommodate the
specification's legal inputs while appropriately restricting outputs. Strengthening a producer guarantee and strengthening a receiver precondition
have opposite consequences. Ownership of a choice is also separate from when information is available.

[INFERRED] Apply that polarity directly to premise pricing. Replacing `A → Q` by `(A ∧ H) → Q` adds a requirement on every caller; it is not a
stronger usable theorem merely because its premise is stronger. Replacing Q by a proven stronger postcondition can preserve old consumers. Dropping
an unused assumption produces a stronger theorem. These three moves need different transport records. Chooser/acceptor identities enforce who can
act; they do not establish alternating simulation. A review response is an environmental input with a continuation, not a witness the repair engine
may select to make its preferred proof pass. Preserve one target revision across that interaction.

[READ R:doc 2 §7; N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, Evidence/Status] The four logical statuses are Witnessed, Open/blocked, Refuted, and Outside this
procedure. They are not synonyms for the five repair outcomes or the five candidate statuses. Nano's `conflict .illTyped` refutes the typing of one
supplied filling; `guardFails` rejects that filling against its guard. Neither proves the target proposition false or the completion space empty.

[INFERRED] Store those three classifications separately. Witnessed requires a checked inhabitant of the exact obligation; Refuted requires a checked
negation or impossibility argument for that same obligation and domain. No retained candidate, a failed tactic, a timeout, an unsupported
translation, and a withdrawn support do not produce Refuted. Keep timeout/unsupported as procedural coverage information alongside the still-open
logical obligation. A claim `P ∨ Collision` can be Witnessed as a literal proposition while blocked as evidence for the intended binding claim.

