[INFERRED] Use three related graphs. The proof graph describes what an elaborated proof uses. The contract graph describes what the proposition and
its definitions mean. The reconstruction graph describes what elaborating the recipe reads, including tactics, instances, simp declarations,
generated files, plugins, and relevant tool configuration. Their edges answer different questions.

[INFERRED] A proof-body edit can change the proof graph without changing the contract graph. A definition changing beneath an unchanged theorem type
can change the contract graph. A simp-set edit can change reconstruction without invalidating an already kernel-checked proof. Conflating these makes
either unsound reuse or needless whole-library re-proving inevitable.

[INFERRED] The Lean exporter should start from both declaration type and value, enumerate constants, follow definition/type dependencies
conservatively, and retain universe and instantiated argument data. Include projection structure identities, generated auxiliaries, recursors,
inductive constructor shapes, and opaque boundaries. Track external dependency versions rather than silently omitting their meaning.

