## What the landed theory actually gives us (rev 3)

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean] An interface names a hole, its context and type, token-backed hypotheses, guard, budget, chooser, and acceptor.
A candidate retains its filling, declared supports, blockers, cost, proposer, and status. Effective supports also include tokens for context
variables the filling actually uses. Tokens have identities and versions.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, judge] Judgment first checks typing, then the interface guard, then live blockers, then missing supports, then
budget. Its statuses distinguish ill-typed or guard-conflicting candidates, pruning, suspension, uncovered cost, and verification. These statuses are
distinct from the five outcomes of repairing a selected filling.

[READ N:Lang/Contextual/Closure.lean, provenance; Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Judge.lean] The provenance representation is a list of
positive-support/negative-blocker pairs, one per candidate passing typing, guard, and cost. Alternatives form a sum; simultaneous supports form a
product. It is independent of which tokens are live, but depends on the environment, candidate set, and checker.

[INFERRED] This is useful signed provenance, not yet an arbitrary symbolic polynomial in all changing facts. In particular, guard failures are
filtered for the supplied environment. Retain the candidate archive as well as its currently computed polynomial, or a later environment change loses
alternatives. Do not flatten a shared proof DAG into exponentially many monomials merely to print this notation.

[READ N:Lang/Proofs/Contextual/Judge.lean, withdraw_never_refutes] Withdrawal changes support availability, not the type or guard calculation; it
does not create a typing/guard conflict. The ill-typed conflict theorem refutes the proposed filling's typing judgment. It does not prove that the
hole's target proposition is false or that no other filling exists.

